html:div.html:details:
Proof.
    We want to maximize

    \begin {align*}
\mathcal {L}(\theta ) &= \mathbb {E}_{x \sim  p_X, y \sim  p_Y}\Big [\log \sigma (f_{\theta }(x)) + \log (1-\sigma (f_{\theta }(y)))\Big ] \\
&= \int  \log \sigma (f_{\theta }(x)) P_X(x) + \int  \log (1-\sigma (f_{\theta }(y))) P_Y(y) \\
&= \int  \log \sigma (f_{\theta }(z)) P_X(z) + \log (1-\sigma (f_{\theta }(z))) P_Y(z)
    \end {align*}

    Since we are maximizing f(s), we can just maximize the integrand i.e.

      \begin {align*}
        \frac {\mathrm {d}}{\mathrm {d}f(z)} \Big [\log \sigma (f_{\theta }(z)) P_X(z) + \log (1-\sigma (f_{\theta }(z))) P_Y(z)\Big ] = 0
      \end {align*}

    Solving,

        \begin {align*}
          P_X(z)\big (1 - \sigma (f(z))\big ) - P_Y(z)\sigma (f(z)) = 0 &\iff  \sigma (f(z)) = \frac {P_X(z)}{P_X(z) + P_Y(z)} \\
          &\iff  f(z) = \log \left (\frac {P_X(z)}{P_Y(z)}\right )
        \end {align*}

fr:mainmatter.html:div:
Example. 
  Loop unrolling cannot make use of the large distance between dependencies for large k below. 
  for (i = k; i < n; i++)
    a[i] += a[i - k];
  For k = 3, Software pipelining can perform something like
  for (i = 3; i < n; i++)
    a[i - 2] += a[i - 2 - k];
    a[i - 1] += a[i - 1 - k];
    a[i] += a[i - k];
  You can imagine how this becomes more useful as k grows large.

Example. 
  There is an actual dependency between instructions here that cannot be circumvented by OoO hardware 
for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
    sum += a[i];
However, this can be re-written at the software level to get some parallelism within loop bodies as:
for (i = 0; i < n; i += 4) {
    s0 += a[i];   s1 += a[i+1];
    s2 += a[i+2]; s3 += a[i+3];
}
sum = (s0 + s1) + (s2 + s3);}

fr:mainmatter.html:p:
Let \rho (s) \in  \mathbb {R}^{|\mathcal {S}|}  be the initial state distribution. Then consider the following LP
    \begin {align*}
\begin {array}{ll} \displaystyle  \max _{V \in  \mathbb {R}^{|\mathcal {S}|}} & \displaystyle  \sum _{s \in  \mathcal {S}} \rho (s) V(s) \\ \text {s.t.} & V(s) \leq  r(s,a) + \gamma  \sum _{s'} P(s' \mid  s,a) \, V(s'), \qquad  \forall  s,a. \end {array}
    \end {align*}
    Translating this into our standard form, we have

\mathbf {c} \in  \mathbb {R}^{|\mathcal {S}|} with c_s = \rho (s)

A \in  \mathbb {R}^{|\mathcal {S}| \times  |\mathcal {A}| \times  |\mathcal {S}|} with A_{(s,a),s'} = -\delta _{s,s'} + \gamma  P(s'|s,a).

    Substituting this in for the dual formulation gives us
    \begin {align*}
& \min _{y(s,a) \geq  0} & & \sum _{s,a} r(s,a) \, y(s,a) \\ & \text {s.t.} & & \sum _{a} y(s,a) - \gamma  \sum _{s',a'} P(s \mid  s',a') \, y(s',a') = \rho (s), \qquad  \forall  s
    \end {align*}
    Here \mathbf {y} can be seen as the discounted occupancy measure. This allows us to recover a randomized optimal policy via
    \mathbb {P}(\pi ^*(s) = a) = \frac {y(s, a)}{\sum _{a} y(s,a)}

